# The remote login's https front (compose.yaml). Caddy's own certificate authority signs for the host (`tls internal`;
# a host with public DNS may take a public certificate instead: drop the tls line). The login app answers only its own
# machine, so each request is handed to it as a page of that machine would send it: the app's own address as its Host,
# and no Origin. The browser's Sec-Fetch-Site still tells the app whether a request came from its own page, and every
# write still needs the app's own header, which no other site's page can send.
#
# Every request handed on is marked as the front's (X-Liminal-Front, set here over whatever a client sent): the front
# shares the app's network and connects from 127.0.0.1 as a program of that machine would, and the mark is how the app
# knows it is not one. So nobody who reaches the front comes in without a password or makes an account without a
# session. Caddy also tells the app the browser came over https (X-Forwarded-Proto: the session cookie is then Secure)
# and the address the front itself was reached from (X-Forwarded-For, what the app's throttle counts sign-ins by),
# writing both over a client's. That address is the browser's own only where Docker hands it through (Docker Engine on
# Linux, the port published wider than 127.0.0.1). Published on 127.0.0.1, behind Docker Desktop, or behind a tunnel or
# a proxy on the host, it is one address for everyone, and the throttle's 30 from one address are then everyone's
# together (the README). Not yet seen in Docker.
{$LIMINAL_REMOTE_HOST}:8443 {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8795 {
		header_up Host 127.0.0.1:8795
		header_up -Origin
		header_up X-Liminal-Front 1
	}
}
